From 8ffce2a08b2a90be2be0ccc020df6a4d5f3f6249 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Agnya <manthram@Pradass-MacBook-Pro.local>
Date: Thu, 10 Sep 2026 14:21:50 +0530
Subject: [PATCH] Fix two real leaks found while verifying the 7 privacy
 toggles are enforced everywhere
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Went through every place another user's data reaches the client
(trip comments, chat participants, trip organizer info, the
users/:id/trips page) to confirm the privacy settings fixed earlier
are actually respected end to end, not just on the one profile-view
endpoint. Found two real gaps:

1. normalizeUserForUI() — the shared display-projection helper used
   for trip organizers, comment authors, and chat participants —
   unconditionally included `email` whenever the input row had one.
   Most callers pass in another user's full row (trip comments,
   accepted trip participants in /api/chat/users), so real email
   addresses were leaking to anyone who could see a comment or share
   a trip chat, regardless of that user's "Show Email" setting.
   Removed email from its output entirely (the one legitimate
   self-view caller, GET /api/user, now adds its own email back onto
   its own response explicitly) so the safe behavior is the only
   behavior, not something every future caller has to remember.

2. GET /api/users/:userId/trips — unconditionally returned real
   firstName/lastName with no check at all of "Show Real Name", and
   never checked profileVisibility, so a user who set their profile
   to Private still had their trips and real name exposed through
   this endpoint. Also returned a user's hidden/inactive trips to
   other viewers, undermining the "Trip Active" visibility toggle.
   Now checks profileVisibility (403 if private and not self), gates
   real name on showRealName, and filters to active trips only for
   non-self viewers.

Also cleaned up two more "Ceylon Traveler" fallback strings, and a
dead isSampleTrip check comparing against an old, never-matching
system@ceylonexpand.com address (the id.startsWith('sample-') check
next to it already covers this correctly).

Flagged but not changed: GET /api/trips/:tripId/interest-requests
deliberately shows the trip organizer a requester's name/email/phone
so they can evaluate a join request — this is a real product
decision (does requesting to join imply consenting to share contact
with that organizer?), not a clear-cut bug like the two above.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
